{id:"destination-definition",dimensionId:"direction",label:"AI DIRECTION",teaching:"Great AI work starts before the answer. This question asks whether you know what you want the finished result to accomplish — not just what words you want AI to produce.",question:"When you ask AI to create something important, how clearly do you define the destination?",order:1,answers:[["extremely-clearly","Extremely clearly","I define the destination precisely.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["very-clearly","Very clearly","I usually know what great looks like.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["fairly-clearly","Fairly clearly","I give AI a clear direction, then refine it.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["somewhat-clearly","Somewhat clearly","I have the destination, but it evolves.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["figure-it-out","I figure it out as I go","The first result often helps me discover what I want.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"communication",dimensionId:"communication",label:"AI COMMUNICATION",teaching:"AI cannot reliably read your mind. The better you explain outcome, context, audience, constraints, and details, the less time you spend correcting misunderstandings.",question:"How clearly do you communicate what you want AI to understand, create, or change?",order:2,answers:[["communication-100","Extremely clearly","I communicate the outcome, context, constraints, and important details.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["communication-85","Very clearly","AI usually understands exactly what I mean.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["communication-70","Fairly clearly","I communicate the main idea and refine details later.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["communication-55","Somewhat clearly","Sometimes AI understands me, sometimes I need to clarify.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["communication-35","I explain it as I go","I discover what I mean through the conversation.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"evaluation",dimensionId:"evaluation",label:"AI EVALUATION",teaching:"One of the biggest differences between casual AI use and AI craftsmanship is judgment. Getting an answer is easy. Knowing whether it is actually good is the higher-value skill.",question:"When AI gives you an answer, how deliberately do you judge whether it is actually good enough?",order:3,answers:[["evaluation-100","Very deliberately","I use clear standards and actively look for weaknesses.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["evaluation-85","Usually deliberately","I regularly inspect quality before accepting the result.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["evaluation-70","Sometimes","I evaluate important outputs but not everything.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["evaluation-55","Occasionally","I usually know when something feels wrong.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["evaluation-35","Rarely","If the answer looks useful, I usually move on.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"iteration",dimensionId:"iteration",label:"AI ITERATION",teaching:"The first answer is rarely the best answer. Excellence comes from seeing what works, identifying what does not, and directing the next version without throwing away the good parts.",question:"When the first AI result is not a 10, what do you normally do?",order:4,answers:[["iteration-100","Diagnose and improve it","I identify exactly why it is not a 10 and direct AI to improve it.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["iteration-85","Refine it","I give feedback and continue improving the result.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["iteration-70","Try another version","I ask AI to make another attempt.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["iteration-55","Make a few changes","I fix obvious issues and move forward.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["iteration-35","Usually accept it","I normally use the first useful result.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"systems",dimensionId:"systems",label:"SYSTEMS THINKING",teaching:"A powerful AI user does not just get one good answer. They notice what worked, preserve it, and turn it into something reusable.",question:"How often do you turn successful AI workflows into reusable systems instead of starting from scratch?",order:5,answers:[["systems-100","Almost always","I capture successful methods and turn them into reusable systems.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["systems-85","Very often","I reuse strong workflows whenever possible.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["systems-70","Sometimes","I reuse some workflows but still start fresh often.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["systems-55","Occasionally","I reuse things only when it is convenient.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["systems-35","Rarely","I usually begin again with each new task.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"context",dimensionId:"direction",label:"DIRECTION",teaching:"Context is the invisible fuel behind a good AI result. Relevant information lets AI reason about the situation instead of guessing.",question:"How consistently do you give AI the context it needs before asking it to solve an important problem?",order:6,answers:[["context-100","Almost always","I make sure AI understands the situation first.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["context-85","Very often","I provide context whenever the task matters.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["context-70","Often","I usually give useful background information.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["context-55","Sometimes","I add context when AI gets confused.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["context-35","Rarely","I usually get straight to the request.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"audience",dimensionId:"communication",label:"COMMUNICATION",teaching:"The same answer can be brilliant for one person and useless for another. Knowing who the work is for changes language, depth, examples, tone, and structure.",question:"How effectively do you explain your intended audience, purpose, and desired outcome to AI?",order:7,answers:[["audience-100","Extremely effectively","Audience and purpose are always part of my direction.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["audience-85","Very effectively","I usually make the audience clear.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["audience-70","Fairly effectively","I explain it when it is important.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["audience-55","Sometimes","I think about the audience after the first result.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["audience-35","Rarely","I mostly let AI decide how to present it.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"verification",dimensionId:"evaluation",label:"EVALUATION",teaching:"AI can sound confident and still be wrong. Verification is the difference between using AI as a source of possibilities and trusting it blindly.",question:"How consistently do you verify important AI-generated information before relying on it?",order:8,answers:[["verification-100","Almost always","I verify important claims before relying on them.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["verification-85","Very often","I verify information when accuracy matters.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["verification-70","Often","I check important information most of the time.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["verification-55","Sometimes","I verify things when something feels questionable.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["verification-35","Rarely","I generally trust useful-looking answers.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"feedback",dimensionId:"iteration",label:"ITERATION",teaching:"Feedback becomes powerful when it changes the next result. The question is not whether you revise — it is whether each revision teaches the process something useful.",question:"How effectively do you use feedback from one AI result to produce a better next result?",order:9,answers:[["feedback-100","Extremely effectively","Every revision gives AI specific information about what to improve.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["feedback-85","Very effectively","I usually give useful feedback and get better results.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["feedback-70","Fairly effectively","I make useful corrections as I go.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["feedback-55","Sometimes","My feedback is often fairly general.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["feedback-35","Not very effectively","I often start over instead of diagnosing the issue.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"workflow",dimensionId:"systems",label:"SYSTEMS THINKING",teaching:"A workflow is a repeatable way of getting something done. Once you discover one that works, you should not have to rediscover it every time.",question:"How often do you design repeatable AI workflows instead of treating every task as a one-off?",order:10,answers:[["workflow-100","Almost always","I deliberately build repeatable workflows.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["workflow-85","Very often","I turn repeated tasks into workflows.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["workflow-70","Sometimes","I build workflows for tasks I do frequently.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["workflow-55","Occasionally","I reuse processes informally.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["workflow-35","Rarely","Most AI work begins from scratch.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"excellence",dimensionId:"direction",label:"DIRECTION",teaching:"Useful and excellent are not the same thing. Excellence requires a standard — a mental picture of what would make the result worth using, sharing, or shipping.",question:"How clearly can you recognize the difference between an AI output that is useful and one that is truly excellent?",order:11,answers:[["excellence-100","Very clearly","I can articulate what separates good from exceptional.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["excellence-85","Clearly","I usually know when something needs another level of refinement.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["excellence-70","Fairly clearly","I can recognize obvious quality differences.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["excellence-55","Somewhat","I mostly judge quality by instinct.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["excellence-35","Not very clearly","If it works, I usually consider it good enough.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"correction",dimensionId:"communication",label:"COMMUNICATION",teaching:"Being misunderstood by AI is not failure. The real skill is recognizing the misunderstanding and communicating the correction clearly enough to get back on track.",question:"How effectively do you correct AI when it misunderstands what you want?",order:12,answers:[["correction-100","Extremely effectively","I diagnose the misunderstanding and redirect precisely.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["correction-85","Very effectively","I usually correct AI quickly.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["correction-70","Fairly effectively","I can usually get things back on track.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["correction-55","Sometimes","I often need several attempts.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["correction-35","Not very effectively","I often abandon the direction and start again.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"red-team",dimensionId:"evaluation",label:"EVALUATION",teaching:"Strong AI users do not only ask what is good. They also ask what could be wrong. That second question is where much of the real quality control happens.",question:"How often do you deliberately look for what could be wrong with an AI-generated result?",order:13,answers:[["redteam-100","Almost always","I actively challenge important outputs.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["redteam-85","Very often","I deliberately look for weaknesses.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["redteam-70","Often","I check important results for obvious problems.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["redteam-55","Sometimes","I inspect results when something feels off.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["redteam-35","Rarely","I usually focus on what is useful.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"preservation",dimensionId:"iteration",label:"ITERATION",teaching:"Improvement should not destroy what already works. High-level AI craftsmanship preserves strengths while deliberately fixing weaknesses.",question:"How intentionally do you preserve what is already working while improving what is not?",order:14,answers:[["preservation-100","Extremely intentionally","I protect what works while targeting specific weaknesses.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["preservation-85","Very intentionally","I usually preserve strong elements during revisions.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["preservation-70","Fairly intentionally","I try not to lose good parts.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["preservation-55","Sometimes","Improvement sometimes changes more than necessary.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["preservation-35","Rarely","I often replace the whole thing when revising.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]},
{id:"capability",dimensionId:"systems",label:"SYSTEMS THINKING",teaching:"The highest level is not having one clever AI trick. It is building capability — knowledge, workflows, tools, standards, and habits that get stronger over time.",question:"How effectively do you turn individual AI successes into a larger repeatable capability?",order:15,answers:[["capability-100","Extremely effectively","I turn successful experiments into durable capability.",100,"#ffd45a","◆"],["capability-85","Very effectively","I regularly capture and reuse what works.",85,"#35e39b","✦"],["capability-70","Fairly effectively","I reuse some successful approaches.",70,"#3ca8ff","△"],["capability-55","Sometimes","I remember useful methods but don't formalize them.",55,"#765cff","✧"],["capability-35","Rarely","Most successes stay as one-off experiences.",35,"#ed42c4","✦"]]}
]},
